## Vec vs Map: Two Tools, Different Jobs

Both store data, but they optimize for different operations. Let's build the same student lookup system two ways and compare.

### The Vec Approach: Ordered by ID

```xiom
type Student = {
  id: Int;
  name: Str;
  grade: Int;
}

fn find_student_vec(students: &Vec[Student], id: Int) -> Option[Str] {
  let i = 0;
  while i < students.len() {
    let s = match students.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => continue };
    if s.id == id {
      return Some(s.name);
    }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return None;
}
```

With Vec, finding a student by ID requires scanning EVERY student. With 5 students, that's fine. With 50,000, every lookup touches up to 50,000 entries.

### The Map Approach: Instant Lookup

```xiom
fn find_student_map(students: &Map[Int, Str], id: Int) -> Option[Str] {
  return students.get(&id);
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let directory: Map[Int, Str] = Map[Int, Str].new();
  directory.insert(101, "Alice");
  directory.insert(102, "Bob");
  directory.insert(103, "Carol");

  let result = find_student_map(&directory, 102);
  io.println(match result {
    Some(name) => "Found: " + name,
    None => "Not found",
  });
  return 0;
}
```

Map lookup is instant — one operation, any size.

### When to Use Which

| Operation | Vec | Map |
|-----------|-----|-----|
| Lookup by index | O(1) | N/A |
| Lookup by key | O(n) | O(1) |
| Iterate in order | O(n) natural | O(n) unordered |
| Add item | O(1) push | O(1) insert |
| Remove by value | O(n) rebuild | O(1) remove |
| Check membership | O(n) scan | O(1) contains |

**Use Vec when**: order matters, you iterate often, or you access by position.
**Use Map when**: you need key-based lookups, deduplication, or fast membership tests.
**Use Set when**: you only care about membership — no values, no order.

